  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Pleasant, uneventful early Fall weather to persist into the\nfirst part of the weekend.\n\nOther than a narrow band of mid-level clouds crossing the\nCommonwealth, another sunny afternoon is in progress. This will\nlead into a mainly clear start to the evening.\n\nLater tonight, a fairly moisture-starved surface cold front will\nmake its way across the state. Although some cloudiness is\nexpected to accompany this system, little to nothing in the way\nof shower activity is anticipated.\n\nThursday should be a breezy and seasonably cool day behind the\naforementioned frontal system, but rain-free. \n\nFriday and Saturday should remain dry, as a narrow ridge of\nsurface high pressure translates across the Commonwealth. By\nSaturday, temperatures should begin to rebound, as low-level \nwarm advection ensues. Highs Saturday afternoon should range \nfrom the upper 60s over the higher terrain, to the mid-upper 70s\nin the Susquehanna Valley.\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Rain chances increase Sunday into Monday, as the remnants of\nIsaias quickly move through the Commonwealth.\n\nOur ensemble prediction systems remain fairly consistent in\nshowing a quick eastward movement of Isaias' remnants and\nassociated moisture from the OH Valley across the Mid-Atlantic\nfrom later this weekend into early Monday. From this early\nvantage point, it looks like the most persistent/concentrated\nrainfall across Central PA will be centered on the Sunday\nafternoon to Sunday night period. Initial indications are that a\ncombination of a short time window for heavier rainfall (most\nlikely 12-18 hours, perhaps even less), plus a welcome recent\nstretch of drier conditions, should significantly mitigate the\npotential for sustained heavy rainfall/flooding. That being\nsaid, we'll continue to watch the progress of this system\nclosely over the coming days. \n\nAt this time, it looks like improving weather can be expected\nwith time on Monday.",
